From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shu Zhao <shuzhao.cs@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 14:44:12 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat: --preempt-sentinel saves and stops before a kill

Slurm preempts with a grace period (a preemptable QOS, say: a
signal, then the kill after the grace period) and warns before the time limit
with --signal. A job script turns either signal into the sentinel file;
the drivers poll it and stop at the earliest safe point:

- an in-flight rollout or eval is abandoned and never trained on. The
  executor cancels the train rollout coroutine, so it stops drawing
  prompts and the data source holds still while it is saved, and calls
  the agent abort hook. The inference controller pauses every engine in
  abort mode: running requests are aborted and new ones held, so agent
  episodes that outlive the rollout cannot reach an engine whose memory
  is being released (SGLang asserts the engine is idle there).
- the latest trained step is saved unless it is already on disk. Right
  after a train step the actor is resident; otherwise (colocated) the
  engines hand the GPUs back and an offloaded actor wakes, and
  save_model first restores the trained weights from the host backup,
  since a woken actor holds fresh pages under --offload-train.
- <sentinel>.done records the rollout id the run is durable up to; the
  job script then requeues or exits, and the resumed run loads it.

The aborted rollout's prompts are skipped on resume (their draws are in
the saved data-source state), as with train_async's prefetched rollout.
PPO critics are saved only inside their train step, so a preempt stop
there warns and reports the last periodic save instead.
